{:edb/db-before-t 1110 :edb/db-after-t 1111 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534423 50 #inst "2026-10-07T02:35:35.260Z" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064003 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." 13194139534423 false] [17592186064003 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9x^2}} \\, \\textrm{d}x.$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064003 1145 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n9x^2 = (3x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1 - 9x^{2}}}dx &= \\frac{9}{4}∫(1)/(\\sqrt{1 - (3x)^{2}})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{4}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{3}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{12}∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - u^{2}}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4} \\cdot \\arcsin u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{4}\\arcsin 3x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 false] [17592186064003 1145 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-u^2}}\\textrm{d}u = \\arcsin(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $9x^2 = (3x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=3x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=3\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 3 \\,\\textrm d u=\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{9}{4\\sqrt{1-9{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(3x)^{2}}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡3x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064004 1100 "Calculate the integral  $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x }.$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064004 1100 "Calculate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139534423 false] [17592186064004 1145 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have\n\n$$\n25x^2 = (5x)^2\n$$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x$. Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫\\frac{3}{2 + 50x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{3}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + 25x^{2}}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}∫(1)/(1 + (5x)^{2})dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{1}{5}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10}∫\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10} \\cdot \\arctan u + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{10}\\arctan 5x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 false] [17592186064004 1145 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{3}{2 + 50x^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int \\dfrac{3}{2(1 + 25x^2)} = \\dfrac 3 2 \\int \\dfrac{1}{1+25x^2}\\,\\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+u^2}\\textrm{d}u =\\arctan u +C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of $u^2$ as the variable, we have $25x^2 = (5x)^2.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=5x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\dfrac 1 5\\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{3}{2+50{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+25{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+(5x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\,\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡5x+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064005 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}$." 13194139534423 false] [17592186064005 1100 "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle{\\int_{0}^{\\sqrt 2} \\dfrac{1}{2+x^2}\\, \\textrm{d}x}.$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064005 1145 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2 + x^{2}}dx &= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1)/(2(1 + x^{2}/2))dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1)/(2(1 + (x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}))dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}(1)/(1 + (x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})dx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution\n\n$$\nu=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u$.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$, and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2 + x^{2}}dx &= \\frac{1}{2}∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1 + u^{2}}du \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\arctan u \\mid _{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\arctan 1 - \\arctan 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{π}{4} - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π\\sqrt{2}}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 false] [17592186064005 1145 "First, we rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+{x}^{2}/2)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2(1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{1+(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this integral now resembles the basic integral for the inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int\\dfrac{1}{1+u^2}\\,\\textrm d u = \\arctan{u} + C.\n$$\n\nWe make the substitution $u=\\dfrac{x}{\\sqrt 2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d u}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt 2}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad \\sqrt 2 \\,\\textrm d u = \\textrm d x.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\sqrt2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u,$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{1}{2+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot \\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{2}}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064014 1100 "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{\\mid 5x \\mid \\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}} =}$" 13194139534423 false] [17592186064014 1100 "${\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{5\\, \\textrm{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^2-1}}  = }$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064014 1145 "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec}$,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\mid u \\mid \\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x$. Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5 \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad 5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫(5dx)/(\\begin{vmatrix}5x & \\sqrt{(5x)^{2} - 1}) &= ∫\\frac{du}{\\mid u\\end{vmatrix}\\sqrt{u^{2} - 1}} \\\\\n&= arcsec(u) + C \\\\\n&= arcsec(5x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 false] [17592186064014 1145 "This resembles the basic integral for $\\textrm{arcsec},$\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|u|\\sqrt{u^2-1}} \\textrm{d}u = \\textrm{arcsec}(u)+C.\n$$\n\nLet $u = 5x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}u}{\\textrm{d}x}=5\n\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\n5\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\textrm{d}u.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{5\\,\\text{d}x}{|5x|\\sqrt{(5x)^{2}-1}} & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}u}{|u|\\sqrt{{u}^{2}-1}} \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(u)+C \\\\  & =\\text{arcsec}(5x)+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064130 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287463 13194139534423 true] [17592186064130 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287470 13194139534423 true] [17592186064130 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287477 13194139534423 true] [17592186064130 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287484 13194139534423 true] [17592186064131 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287491 13194139534423 true] [17592186064131 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287526 13194139534423 true] [17592186064131 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287533 13194139534423 true] [17592186064131 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287551 13194139534423 true] [17592186064131 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287558 13194139534423 true] [17592186064132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287512 13194139534423 true] [17592186064132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287519 13194139534423 true] [17592186064132 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287540 13194139534423 true] [17592186064162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287498 13194139534423 true] [17592186064162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287505 13194139534423 true] [17592186064162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287544 13194139534423 true] [17592186064162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287565 13194139534423 true] [17592186064162 1020 #edb/ref 17592186287572 13194139534423 true] [17592186064460 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534423 true] [17592186064460 1100 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064460 1100 "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2 - x^{2}}}dx$." 13194139534423 false] [17592186064460 1145 "Let's rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot (1-{x}^{2}/2)}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}/2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have ${(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(x/\\sqrt{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\cdot (\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u{|}_{0}^{\\sqrt{2}/2} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064461 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534423 true] [17592186064461 1100 "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064461 1100 "Evaluate the integral $∫_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2 + 8x^{2}}dx$." 13194139534423 false] [17592186064461 1145 "Note that we can rewrite this integral as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{1+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis integral resembles the basic integral for inverse tangent,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nBefore substituting, we use the table below to change the limits from $x$ to $u.$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $0$ | $14$ |\n\nUsing the above, we can now write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{9}{2+8{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{7}\\frac{1}{1+(2x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{14}\\frac{1}{1+{u}^{2}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{14}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{1+{u}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡u{|}_{0}^{14} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)-\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(14)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064563 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534423 true] [17592186064563 1100 "$\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186064563 1100 "What is $∫\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1 - 4x^{2}}}dx$?" 13194139534423 false] [17592186064563 1145 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $4{x}^{2}=(2x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=2x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-4{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(2x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(2x)}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287463 1017 #edb/ref 17592186287464 13194139534423 true] [17592186287463 1089 "q-114023" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287463 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534423 true] [17592186287463 1100 "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287463 1113 #uuid "71e110b8-dac7-55ea-9c66-c30aaca2c79e" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287463 1145 "This integral resembles the basic integral for inverse sine,\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(u)+C.\n$$\n\nThe only difference is that instead of ${u}^{2}$ as the variable, we have $36{x}^{2}=(6x)^{2}.$\n\nSo, let's substitute $u=6x.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=6\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{1-36{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-(6x)^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{u}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡u+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287465 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287466 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287467 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287468 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1000 #edb/ref 17592186287469 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1041 "selection" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1104 #edb/ref 17592186287465 13194139534423 true] [17592186287464 1110 #uuid "0b9fefbf-0fd1-58ea-a9e5-d4f208246cd5" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287465 1034 "\\frac{1}{36}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287465 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534423 true] [17592186287465 1111 #uuid "db06961f-0dd2-5efc-be06-4e0611964fe0" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287466 1034 "6\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287466 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534423 true] [17592186287466 1111 #uuid "5c97499b-3e30-5cef-a80a-c25520c3e23f" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287467 1034 "36\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534423 true] [17592186287467 1111 #uuid "cf93e1a6-1bbf-5a81-8c7b-5fe67a0988c4" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287468 1034 "\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(6x)+C" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534423 true] [17592186287468 1111 #uuid "352bf025-f82e-50ad-8c77-25bd6188beb3" 13194139534423 true] [17592186287469 10